N2  - Background: Despite advances in interventional approaches and more effective chemotherapy regimens, surgical resection of biliary tract tumors remains the gold standard of curative treatment. Over the past years, epidemiological data indicate a worldwide increase in the incidence of this comparatively rare but lethal disease. Cholangiocarcinomas are subdivided based on their location within the biliary tract: The surgical treatment of tumors located within the liver (intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma [iCCA]) differs significantly from tumors located within the extrahepatic biliary tree (extrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, eCCA). The latter can be further subdivided with respect to the involvement of the confluence of the left and right hepatic bile ducts and the common hepatic duct (“Klatskin tumor” or perihilar cholangiocarcinoma [pCCA]), or the common bile duct (distal cholangiocarcinoma [dCCA]). Objectives: This article provides an overview of the principles of surgical treatment as well as current developments and controversies. Methods: We performed a review of the literature using the PubMed database. Results: Often advanced tumor growth at the time of diagnosis justifies staging laparoscopy. Aggressive surgical resections, including laparoscopic resection and modern strategies to induce hypertrophy, should be performed in specialized centers. Liver transplantation for CCA in Germany outside of clinical trials is negligible. Conclusions: Technological developments and recent findings are the basis for new surgical opportunities and controversies.
