N2  - Aim: Recent studies have suggested liver transplantation is a better treatment option for resectable early-stage peri-hilar cholangiocarcinoma (pCCA). The aim of this study was to investigate outcomes following resection of early-stage pCCA in the UK to inform UK decisions regarding liver transplantation for pCCA. A secondary aim was to identify clinicopathological variables that influenced survival. Method: Patients undergoing resection for pCCA between January 2014 and July 2022 across 22 UK centres were included. Early-stage pCCA was defined as tumour size <3cm with no nodal disease (N0). Collated data included demographics, surgical procedure, use of chemotherapy, histopathology and survival outcome. Results: Of the 450 patients included, 138 patients underwent resection for early-stage pCCA. In the early-stage pCCA group, CD>IIIa morbidity was 39.1%(n=54) and 90-day mortality was 10.1%(n=14). Sixty-four (46.4%) patients received adjuvant chemotherapy, but this was reduced in those with CD>IIIa morbidity (n=17,31.5%). Early-stage tumours had a significantly lower rate of vascular invasion (n=57,41.3%) and R1 margin (n=46,33.3%) compared to later-stage pCCA [62.2%(n=194) and 54.2%(n=169) respectively, p<0.001). The median disease-free (31 versus 20 months) and overall (43 versus 26 months) survival was significantly better in patients with early-stage pCCA compared to more advanced tumours (p<0.001). Male gender (p=0.039) and Grade B/C Post Hepatectomy Liver Failure (PHLF, p=0.010) were associated with significantly worse disease-free survival, while biliary drainage (p=0.013), Grade B/C PHLF (p<0.001) and vascular invasion (p=0.030) were associated with significantly poorer overall survival. Conclusion: Resection of early-stage pCCA tumours is associated with good clinical and survival outcomes in experienced, centralized HPB centres.
