N2  - The aim of the study is to present an experience of 19 Klatskin tumors resected between January 1995 - December 2002 in the Center of General Surgery and Liver Transplantation (Fundeni Clinical Institute - Bucharest). The main presenting symptoms were: weight loss (94.75%), jaundice (89.74%) and epigastric pain (78.94%). The preoperative work-up included ultrasound (all patients), CT scan, ERCP and cholangio-MRI. The tumors were divided into four anatomic types, according to the modified Bismuth-Corlette classification (type II lesions - 26.31%; type III lesions - 52.62%; type IV lesions - 21.05%). Assessment of resectability was established by preoperative investigations but in most of the cases the intraoperative exploration was determinant. Operative procedures included resection of the biliary tract associated or not with liver resection. Postoperative morbidity was 15.78% (3 patients): stenosis of the biliary anastomosis, external biliary fistula, intraabdominal abscess. Postoperative mortality was 10.52% (2 patients, with acute liver failure). When performed by experienced surgical teams, aggressive resection for Klatskin tumors has an acceptable morbidity and mortality rate and it represents the only chance for cure. The mortality and morbidity is proportional to the extent of the surgical procedure (higher when accompanied by extended liver resections).
