N2  - Combined hepatocellular-cholangiocarcinoma is a rare malignancy comprising less than 5% of primary hepatic carcinomas. This is an entity which exhibits characteristics of both hepatocellular carcinoma as well as cholangiocarcinoma. In this case, we see an 48 year old male with no significant past medical history who presents to the ED with significant unintentional weight loss, decreased appetite, and right lower quadrant abdominal pain for 3 months. No history of Hepatitis infection. Denied smoking, alcohol, or illicit substance use. Vital signs were within normal limits, and physical exam was grossly unremarkable. Initial laboratory analysis revealed anemia to Hg 9.3, AST/ALT 50/36, Tb/Db 0.5/0.2, Albumin 3.0, INR 1.1, AFP 2.1, CEA 1.2. A CT abdomen/ pelvis was obtained revealing a large 16.5 cm mass in the right hepatic lobe, a second 3.2 cm mass in the caudate lobe, and evidence of thrombus formation in the distal IVC and left renal vein. The patient was initiated on a heparin drip for management of the thrombi and admitted for further work up of the lesions. An IR guided biopsy of the hepatic mass was performed. The results were consistent with combined hepatocellular-cholangiocarcinoma with stem cells features. Combined hepatocellular-cholangiocarcinoma is a rare primary hepatic cancer. The incidence of which varies between 0.4 to 4.7%. The defining characteristic of CHC (according to the WHO) is tissue demonstrating both hepatocellular and biliary components, confirmed histopathologically on biopsy. Studies have identified risk factors to include male gender, cirrhosis, hepatitis, familial history of hepatic cancers, alcohol consumption, and diabetes mellitus. The use of imaging modalities alone in diagnosing CHC is difficult due to the sharing of characteristics of both HCC and CC. Tumor markers such as AFP and CA-19-9 may aid in the clinical diagnosis of CHC. The consensus for recommended treatment is major or minor hepatic resection with or without lymph node dissection. There may also be a role of liver transplantation in these patients. Unfortunately, the overall prognosis for CHC patients is very poor with 3-year survival rates ranging between 10.5%-47% and 5-year survival rates ranging between 9.2%-40%. It is important for clinicians to be aware of combine hepatocellular-cholangiocarcinoma. It is a unique entity which has significant overlap with other forms of primary hepatic malignancies.
