N2  - Purpose/Objective(s): We report an institutional experience for treatment of hilar/extrahilar cholangiocarcinoma (CCA). We analyzed the impact of 3 treatment pathways on clinical outcomes: neoadjuvant chemoradiation followed by orthotopic liver transplantation (OLT), surgical resection and adjuvant CRT (aCRT), and definitive CRT (dCRT). Materials/Methods: Patients treated between 1998-2019 were retrospectively reviewed. Treatment included external beam radiation concurrently with continuous-infusion 5-flurouracil, capecitabine, gemcitabine, or cisplatin. Overall survival (OS), local progression-free survival (LPFS), and disease-free survival (DFS) were calculated using Kaplan-Meier estimates and log-rank test. Univariate and multivariate Cox regression models were used to assess the relationships between clinical and treatment factors with outcome. Results: Sixty-five (65) patients (20 OLT vs. 16 aCRT vs. 29 dCRT) with median age of 65.0 (range: 27.0–84.0) years at diagnosis met inclusion criteria. Median RT dose was 50.4 Gy (range: 7.2-62.0 Gy) in each patient cohort. Seventeen (26%) patients had ulcerative colitis; 19 (29%) patients had primary sclerosing cholangitis. Death occurred at the end of follow-up in 40 (62% [6 OLT, 10 aCRT, and 24 dCRT]) patients. From the end of RT, median follow-up for survival was 19.1 (range: 0.8-164.8) months, with medians for OLT, aCRT, and dCRT groups being 38.6, 24.3, and 9.0 months, respectively. Survival rates at 3 and 5 years were 78% and 69% in the OLT group, 49% and 38% in the aCRT group, and 19% and 6% in the dCRT group, respectively. Compared to the dCRT group, the aCRT group [HR = 0.11 (95% CI: 0.04-0.29)] and OLT group [HR = 0.29 (95% CI: 0.14-0.60)] had significantly improved OS (P<0.001). Log-rank tests of 5-year PFS (94% OLT vs 67% AdRT vs. 24% CRT, P<0.0001) and 5-year DFS (100% OLT vs 73% AdRT vs. 52% CRT, P = 0.01) survival rates also significantly favored OLT and aCRT over dCRT. Univariate analysis demonstrated treatment strategy (OLT vs. aCRT vs. dCRT), negative lymph node status, smaller tumor size, younger patient age, and baseline CA 19-9 < 145 were significantly associated with improved clinical outcomes (all P-values < 0.05). Conclusion: In patients with extrahepatic/hilar cholangiocarcinoma, outcomes were superior for those who underwent neoadjuvant CRT followed by OLT or aCRT after surgery compared to those patients treated only with dCRT. Given the retrospective study design, these outcomes are influenced by patient selection bias. Nevertheless, our data supports a strategy where patients are first evaluated for the transplant protocol or surgery resection whenever possible. Improvements are needed in the delivery of dCRT through technical advances or novel combined modality therapies.
