N2  - Background: For the treatment of hilar cholangiocarcinoma and hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) in the caudate lobe, recognition of the vascular anatomy of the caudate lobe is the key to safely and effectively performing surgery and transcatheter arterial chemoembolization (TACE). The aim of this study was to precisely analyze the caudate artery as well as the relationship between the caudate arteries, the arterial plexus at the hilar plate, and the hilar bile duct. Patients and Methods: Reconstructed three-dimensional (3D) images from 50 out of 93 patients from CT during hepatic arteriography (CTHA) were analyzed. A 3D image analysis system (SYNAPSE VINCENT; Fujifilm Medical, Tokyo) was used for 3D image reconstruction. We defined the right branch (Ir) as the branch to the right part of the caudate lobe (the caudate process and the paracaval portion) and the left branch (Il) as the branch to the left part of the caudate lobe (Spiegel lobe). Additionally, we defined the CA as the artery connecting the right hepatic artery (RHA), left hepatic artery (LHA), and segmental hepatic artery. Results: The caudate artery was divided into 3 types (FIGURE. 1): an independent branch of the hepatic artery (Type 1); the common tract formed by Ir and Il (Type 2); and an arterial branch from the CA (Type 3). The CA was recognized in 25 (50%) of 50 patients. The total number of CA was 26. A total of 66 caudate arteries arose from 26 CAs, of which 23 were Ir and 43 were Il. There was a total of 65 arteries to the hilar bile duct observed in 40 patients, and 24 (37%) of these 65 arteries to the hilar bile duct originated from the caudate artery or CA. Conclusions: The 3D imaging reconstructed by CTHA showed that the caudate artery plays an important role not only in the arterial collateral system of the liver but also in the blood supply to the hilar bile duct. The recognition of this vascular anatomy is clinically important for performing liver resection for hilar cholangiocarcinoma, living-related liver transplantation, and effective TACE for HCC.
