N2  - Introduction: Prompt diagnosis of extrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma (eCCA) is key to improving survival. Prevalence and factors contributing to diagnostic delay in patients with denovo eCCA have yet to be explored. This study aims to evaluate diagnostic delays in patients with eCCA. Methods: This single center retrospective study included patients with perihilar or distal, de-novo CCA between January 2015 to January 2023. Patient demographics, presenting symptoms, time to physician visits, lab data, and imaging data were collected from electronic medical records. Diagnostic delay was defined as more than 60 days from symptom onset to diagnosis. Analysis was performed using R v4.1.3 with results presented as mean (standard deviation), median (interquartile range), or n (%). Results: The final cohort included 248 patients with a mean age of 63.6 years with 154 (62.1%) being males. A majority (n=165, 66.5%) presented with obstructive biliary symptoms (jaundice ± abdominal pain, pruritus, and/or acholic stools). The median time from symptom onset to seeing a physician was 11 days (6-17 days) with a median time to diagnosis of 45 days (16-49 days). Primary care was the first contact for most patients (n=209, 84.3%) with ultrasound being the most common initial imaging (n=131, 52.8%). A delay in diagnosis was noted in 101 total patients (40.7%). Patients without obstructive biliary symptoms were more likely to experience a diagnostic delay (53.1% vs. 34.5%, p<0.01). Diagnostic delay was associated with a longer time from symptom onset to first provider visit (15 [2-39] vs. 10 [2-17] days, p<0.01). One hundred nineteen (48.8%) patients underwent curative resection/ liver transplantation. Patients with obstructive biliary symptoms had higher baseline CA19-9 levels (196 [61.5-1095.5] vs. 71 [18.0-279.0] u/ml, p<0.01) and were less likely to undergo curative treatment (42.8% vs. 60.2%, p<0.01) compared to those without obstructive symptoms. In patients without obstructive symptoms, time from consultation to imaging was longer in patients who were ineligible for resection/transplantation (median time 2 [0-28] vs. 0 [0-2] days, p=0.03). Conclusion: Individuals without obstructive biliary symptoms more commonly experienced diagnostic delay for de novo eCCA, largely due to a delay in initial medical consultation. A longer interval from the first consultation to imaging correlated with reduced eligibility for curative treatment in these patients. Conversely, patients presenting with obstructive symptoms receive faster diagnoses but often have more advanced disease with lower likelihood for curative treatment. A high index of suspicion and prompt imaging are essential to mitigate delays in appropriate care.
