N2  - Introduction: Neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy with orthotopic liver transplantation (OLT) is effective treatment for perihilar cholangiocarcinoma (pCCA). Although visible common bile duct (CBD) involvement is an exclusion, patients with primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC) and microscopic CBD involvement detected by frozen section or endoscopic brushings prior to neoadjuvant therapy have undergone pancreatoduodenectomy (PD) or planned en-bloc hepatopancreatoduodenectomy (HPD) during OLT. Method: We reviewed pCCA patients treated with neoadjuvant therapy and OLT with PD between 1993 – 2021 with aims to determine survival, morbidity and mortality. Survival was calculated by Kaplan-Meier method. Results: Four hundred eight patients began neoadjuvant therapy and 266 (173 with pCCA arising in PSC, 93 with de novo pCCA) underwent OLT. Twenty-four patients underwent pancreatoduodenectomy (11 HPD, 13 PD). Nine patients (38%) had minor complications, 10 (42%, 13% in HPD group vs 29% in PD group) had major complications, and 13 (54 %) developed a pancreatic fistula (7 grade B, 6 grade C). Two patients (8%) died within 90 days. Median follow-up was 33 months [10-67]. Eight patients (33%) developed recurrences. Median time to recurrence was 32 months [12-40]. One, 3-, and 5-year survival after OLT and PD were 82%, 61%, and 43% compared to 93%, 82%, and 75% for 151 patients with pCCA arising in PSC who did not require PD (p=0.008). Conclusions: OLT with PD is associated with significant perioperative morbidity and mortality but seems reasonable when CBD involvement is encountered during OLT. We remain cautious about planned HPD for patients with CBD involvement detected prior to neoadjuvant therapy.
