N2  - Background: Surgical resection remains the only potentially curative treatment strategy for perihilar cholangiocarcinoma (phCCA), but vascular invasion often challenges the aim of obtaining clear resection margins. Therefore, vascular resection and reconstruction represent a pivotal aspect in the surgical management of phCCA to achieve optimal oncological outcomes. Methods: In this retrospective single-center analysis, we assessed 32 patients with phCCA, who underwent surgical resection with vascular reconstruction from 2004 to 2024. Data on various perioperative parameters were collected, including the type of vascular reconstruction performed, incidence of post-hepatectomy liver failure (PHLF), postoperative morbidity, and 90-day mortality rates. Additionally, oncological outcomes were also examined. Results: A total of 32 patients with phCCA were included in the analysis, with a median age of 62 years. The cohort consisted of 20 males (62.5%) and 12 females (37.5%). Most patients (81.3%) underwent portal vein reconstruction, while 12.5% had hepatic artery reconstruction and 6.3% received combined vascular reconstructions. The frequency of vascular reconstructions increased over time, with 50% of the cases occurring between 2011 and 2020 and 12.5% between 2021 and 2024. Positive lymph node status (N1) was noted in 53.1%. Complications categorized by the Clavien-Dindo classification indicated that 31.35% experienced significant morbidity (≥grade III). In particular, complications associated with vascular reconstruction, such as vascular occlusion/ thrombosis, pseudoaneurysms and bleeding, were documented in 18.8% of cases (n=6). Notably, PHLF grade B/C occurred in 25% of patients. The 90-day mortality rate was recorded at 9.4% with all 3 patients suffering from PHLF. At a median follow-up period of 18 months, local recurrences were present in 5 patients (15.6%), either at the hilar region (n=2; 6.3%) or at the parenchymal resection margin (n=3; 9.4%). Distant recurrences were reported in 12 cases (37.5%). These included retroperitoneal lymph nodes in 2 (6.3%), intrahepatic recurrences in 5 (15.6%), and peritoneal metastases in 5 patients (15.6%). Adjuvant therapy was administered in 56.3% of patients, with gemcitabine-based regimens being the most common treatment (38.9%). In addition, our findings indicate an estimated 1-year OS rate of 85.3% and an estimated 3-year OS rate of 44.5%, while the estimated 1-year RFS rate was 76.4% and the estimated 3-year RFS rate was 16.3%. A strong tendency towards significance for poorer OS (p=0.053) in patients with R1 resection status (mOS 24.2 months, 95% CI: 11.201, 37.279) was observed when compared to those with R0 (mOS 42.3 months, 95% CI: 10.135, 74.245), while nodal status did not exhibit a significant association. Conclusion: Referencing recent benchmark analyses, vascular resection and reconstruction for phCCA appears to be associated with comparable perioperative and oncological outcomes to standard resections without the need of vascular resection. However, almost 20% of patients suffered from morbidity associated with vascular reconstruction that required complication-specific interventions. PHLF remains the main cause of death in these patients. In patients with vascular resection, distant recurrence becomes the main cause of treatment failure, highlighting the need for effective systemic therapy. Meanwhile, in patients with R1 resected phCCA, liver transplantation should be considered as a viable treatment option to improve long-term outcomes. [Formula presented]
