N2  - Liver Transplantation (LT) is an effective treatment for patients with unresectable hilar cholangiocarcinoma (hCCA), particularly those with chronic liver disease. It has shown significant long-term benefits for unresectable perihilar cholangiocarcinoma (pCCA), making it a viable option for these challenging cases. Neoadjuvant therapy enhances local and distant control while reducing the risk of metastasis. However, its use before LT raises concerns about vascular complications. Patients without cirrhosis often demonstrate longer survival, while those with cirrhosis are assessed based on their functional liver remnant (FLR). If the FLR is below 40%, resection is unsuitable, but the patient may be eligible for transplantation. A 55-year-old male with diabetes, hypertension, ASA II status, and 20% right coronary artery stenosis presented with abdominal bloating, itching, a CA19-9 level of 174, GGT of 1722 U/L, ALT of 272 U/L, AST of 169 U/L, and direct bilirubin of 2.45 mg/dL. He had no history of alcohol use or hepatitis. MRI and CT showed a 3 cm mass extending from the perihilar bile duct to the left bile duct (Bismuth Type 3B). Liver volume was calculated as 2106 cc, with no other pathologies observed. A left hepatectomy was planned. During surgery, minimal ascites and a cirrhotic liver appearance were observed. A true-cut biopsy of the liver parenchyma revealed Grade 2 macrosteatosis, confirming cirrhosis. It was determined that the remaining liver volume would be insufficient after hepatectomy. Interventional radiology accessed the left bile duct intraoperatively and performed a brush cytology procedure, confirming cholangiocarcinoma. Palpable lymph nodes removed from the hilar region were negative for malignancy. The patient was referred to the transplant board for evaluation. Postoperative CT on day 10 revealed intraperitoneal ascites and tumor growth. Neoadjuvant therapy was initiated. Monthly MRI follow-ups showed tumor shrinkage to 1.5 cm. Following this, a decision was made to proceed with liver transplantation. A living donor liver transplantation was performed without complications. The patient’s postoperative recovery was uneventful, and he was discharged on day 11. At the 4-month follow-up, he remains in stable condition without complications.
