N2  - Liver transplantation (LTx) is standard of care in patients with end stage liver failure. In cases of primary or secondary malignant tumours in the liver, radical surgery (R0 resection) is offering potential curative treatment. Conventional liver surgery is however limited by the number, size and location of the tumours, their relation to major vascular structures as well as the remnant liver volume after resection. Liver transplantation in the setting of liver tumour provides “superradical” surgery where many of the limitations met in conventional resections can be addressed. The LTx concept can be utilized in two distinct manners; either as an allotransplantation with replacement of the patients liver, or by using transplant-surgical techniques for organ preservation and vascular control by operation on the patients own liver. The immunosuppression required for allotransplantation is a major factor limiting this approach in the treatment of malignant liver tumours. By applying rigorous selection criteria, a subset of patients with primary hepatic tumours such as hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) and hilar cholangiocarcinoma (CCA) can be given an excellent survival (> 70% at 5 years). A similar survival benefit can be shown for other rare primary liver tumours such as malignant epitheloid hemangioendothelioma. A similar approach might be valid for a carefully selected group with metastatic disease confined to the liver. Patients with neuroendocrine tumours are in this category. Ongoing studies are currently exploring transplantation in the setting of colorectal liver metastasis. The utilization of transplantation techniques that facilitates organ preservation for an extended period and optimal vascular control can vastly extend the limits for operability, thus offering surgical therapy to patients otherwise deemed as inoperable. Intraoperative cooling and veno-venous bypass enables reconstruction of the liver vasculature under total vascular exclusion. This can be further developed to autotransplantation techniques by taking the liver partially (ante-situm) or completely (ex-situ) out from its position to gain surgical access to tumours and structures that otherwise would be too hazardous to approach.
