N2  - Introduction: Neuroendocrine tumors (NET) of the hilar bile duct are very rare tumors. Here we have described a case of NET arising from the bile duct confluence mimicking a hilar cholangiocarcinoma. Case report: A 42-year-old lady presented with intermittent pain in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen associated with nausea, anorexia and weight loss. There was no history of jaundice, pruritus or of symptoms related to hormone/vasoactive peptide hypersecretion. Axial imaging demonstrated a hilar mass at the porta hepatis compressing the left and common hepatic duct and the proximal common bile duct with intrahepatic biliary radical dilatation and involvement of the right hepatic artery. PET scan showed evidence of active disease only at the porta. A left hepatectomy with bile duct resection and hepatic artery reconstruction was performed. Histopathological examination of the mass revealed a NET (WHO grade 2; Ki-67 index: 4%) with clear margins and negative regional lymph nodes. Nine months following the surgery the patient is disease free. Discussion: Primary biliary NETs are very rare, mainly owing to the fact that the enterochromaffin cells from which they arise are very sparse in the biliary system. Jaundice (60%), abdominal pain (43%) and pruritus (19%) are the most common symptoms. Symptoms related to hormone/ vasoactive peptide secretion are seen in <10% of cases. NETS are more common in females (F:M ratio of 1.6:1) at a mean age of approximately 47 years. Surgery (in the form of resection or liver transplantation) is the only chance at cure.
