N2  - Background: Periampullary carcinomas, including ampullary, duodenal, and extrahepatic cholangiocarcinomas, are aggressive malignancies primarily treated with surgical resection, typically pancreaticoduodenectomy (PD). (1) Despite improved resectability rates compared to pancreatic ductal adenocarcinoma (PDAC), 5-year survival following resection remains poor, ranging from 30% to 50%. (2) Ampullary carcinomas, however, have a better prognosis with a 5-year survival rate of 45% in resected patients. (3) Neoadjuvant therapy (NAT) has gained interest due to potential benefits similar to those in PDAC, including earlier treatment of micrometastatic disease and improved locoregional control. (2) Method: We retrospectively analysed 60 patients who underwent PD at our centre from 2012 to 2022. Clinicopathological data and survival outcomes were obtained. The time-to-events data were compared using Kaplan-Meier methods. A univariate Cox proportional hazards regression was performed to evaluate factors associated with overall survival (OS) and generate hazard ratios (HR). Results: 60 patients underwent surgical resection of perimampullary carcinomas. 64% were male and 36% female. The median age was 74.5 (IQR 66-81). 5 year overall survival was 53.3% (95% CI 0.421-0.676). In univariate Cox proportional hazards regression analysis, features including nodal positivity were found to be associated with worse OS (HR 2.56 95% CI 1.16-5.64 p=0.02). Conclusion: Despite significant progress in cancer treatment and research, addressing rare cancers like ampullary cancer continues to pose substantial challenges due to the absence of a well-defined, evidence-based standard treatment protocol. Our study demonstrated a 5-year overall survival rate of 53.3%, which is notably higher than previously cited survival for periampullary carcinomas. This may be attributed to advancements in surgical techniques, patient selection, or perioperative care over the past decade.
