N2  - Background: Cholangiocarcinomas are an anatomically and genetically highly heterogenous group of malignant tumors of the bile duct. They are diagnosed late and share a limited outcome. Objective: The aim of the current study was to provide an overview of the different types of cholangiocarcinomas and their specific therapeutic algorithms. Materials and methods: A PubMed search was performed to present current evidence-based recommendations and the authors’ personal experiences with treating cholangiocarcinoma patients were considered. Results: Adequate care of cholangiocarcinoma patients is a complex multidisciplinary task. Due to a lack of early detection methods, cholangiocarcinomas are often diagnosed late, when tumors are already advanced. Depending on tumor location, extended liver or pancreatic resections are necessary to give patients a chance of long-term survival. Multimodal treatment algorithms, multistage resection strategies, and minimally invasive or robotic surgery can extend the resectability of cholangiocarcinomas in selected centers. Neoadjuvant treatment concepts are being evaluated and appear to improve resectability and outcome. In strictly selected patients, liver transplantation after neoadjuvant radiochemotherapy also improves outcome. Conclusion: Resectability remains subjective and differs significantly between clinics. Before initiation of palliative care, all patients should be presented and discussed in specialized high-volume (transplant) centers. Telemedical tumor boards could facilitate patient management and should be widely implemented.
