N2  - In conclusion, orthotopic liver transplantation can be used to cure some patients with hepatic malignancy and will provide prolonged survival and symptomatic relief for others. Indeed, a postoperative 1-year survival of over 30% is better than is currently being achieved for pancreatic and oesophageal malignancy, and it has to be remembered that patients undergoing liver grafting are those who are not suitable for hepatic resection. To further reduce the incidence of tumour recurrence more attempts must be made (i) to detect the primary tumour before extrahepatic spread occurs by regular screening of all cirrhotic patients; (ii) to improve detection of extrahepatic malignancy before transplantation; and (iii) to improve adjuvant therapy, to be used either prophylactically following transplantation or if the tumour recurs.
