N2  - Introduction: Neoadjuvant chemoradiotherapy with liver transplantation (RT+LT) is effective for unresectable perihilar cholangiocarcinoma (pCCA). Meanwhile, liver resection with vascular resection (LR+VR) challenges the concept of unresectability in pCCA. This study evaluates the outcomes of RT+LT, LR+VR, and without VR (LR w/o VR) at a single center. Method: De novo pCCA patients undergoing resection or RT+LT were included (1993-2023). Overall (OS) and recurrence-free survival (RFS) were evaluated using as-treated (AT) and intention-to-treat (ITT) approaches. Patients were matched 1:2 on age, sex, BMI, ECOG, nodal-negative status, tumor size ≤3cm, CA 19-9 levels, and treatment period. Results: 243 LR (AT: 207; LR+VR: 37, LR w/o VR: 170) and 93 RT+LT patients (AT: 75) were included (Fig.1A). For all-comers, 5-year OS was similar between RT+LT and LR w/o VR but superior to LR+VR in AT (50%/54%/27%). In matched cohorts, LR w/o VR had the most favorable OS, while a tendency towards superior OS was shown when comparing RT+LT with LR+VR in AT. Comparable incidences were observed between LR w/o VR, LR+VR and RT+LT regarding grade≥ III complications (35%/49%/44%) and 90d mortality (7%/8%/4%), whereas R1 resection rates differed significantly (17%/32%/0%). RFS for RT+LT was significantly better than LR w/o VR and LR+VR for all-comers. However, when accounting for competing risks RFS was similar between RT+LT and LR (Fig.1B-E). Conclusion: RT+LT demonstrated comparable results to LR in nodal-negative ≤3cm tumors, supporting its role in unresectable pCCA. Despite worse outcomes, LR+VR is feasible in centers with advanced expertise, particularly for non-transplant candidates. [Formula presented]
