N2  - Background: Mayo Clinic has reported excellent outcomes after liver transplantation (LT) for perihilar cholangiocarcinoma (phCCA). It raises a question whether LT can play a role also in resectable phCCA. This study aims to estimate if LT potentially improves outcomes for resectable phCCA, by comparing our results of liver resection with reported outcomes after LT for phCCA. Material and Methods: Fifty-five patients with phCCA undergoing liver resection with extrahepatic bile duct resection between 2006 and 2016 were analyzed. Results: Five-year overall survival rate (OSR) for R0/R1 (n=43/12) patients was 50%/25%. R1 patients without positive distal ductal margin (n=7) would theoretically have had a chance of R0 by LT. However, among these 7 patients, recurrences were observed only in two patients and the mode of recurrence was distant metastases in both, which would not have been prevented even with LT. Twenty-seven patients satisfied the Mayo Criteria for LT (negative lymph node metastasis and negative cystic duct involvement) and their 5-year OSR was 60%, which is comparable to reported 5-year OSR after LT for de novo phCCA. Among 10 patients who relapsed, 4 patients had local recurrence, which would have been possibly prevented by LT. Among the 27 patients, 10 patients would have been deemed unresectable by the Mayo Criteria but their 5-year OSR was 68%, which was not inferior to that in the resectable patients (56%). Conclusion: LT unlikely improves postoperative outcomes for resectable phCCA. Our criteria for resectability seemed appropriate.
