N2  - Introduction: Liver resection (LR) with hepatic artery reconstruction (HAR) and transplantation (OLT) are therapeutic options for locally-advanced perihilar cholangiocarcinoma (pCCA). We aimed to compare outcomes of LR versus OLT. Method: Patients undergoing LR with HAR from 10 western centres were compared to an OLT cohort of patients both undergoing or not neo-adjuvant chemo-radiation (NACR). Outcomes were major and vascular complications, 90-days mortality, overall (OS) and recurrence-free survival (RFS). Results: 109 patients, 60 LR patients, 22 OLT no-NACR and 27 OLT NACR, were included. LR patients were older and had less frequently Bismuth IV tumours. R1 (49.2% vs 6.5%, p<0.001) and N1 (54.2% vs 32.4%, p=0.058) were more frequent in LR patients. No differences were found between LR and OLT in major (40% vs 46.9%, p=0.56) and vascular complications (23.3% vs 28.6%, p=0.66), but NACR was an independent risk factor for vascular complications (OR 2.63, 95%CI 1.03-6.70, p=0.043). PHLF was more frequent in LR, severe biliary complications were more frequent in OLT no-NACR compared to LR (40.9% vs 16.7%, p=0.036). 90 days mortality (15% for LR vs 10.2% for OLT, p=0.57) and 5-years OS (HR 0.68, 95%CI 0.40-1.17, p=0.17) were similar, OLT had better 5-years RFS (HR 0.52, 95%CI 0.29-0.96, p=0.035). R1 resection (HR 2.07, 95%CI 1.03-4.18, p=0.041) and perineural invasion (HR 3.64, 95% CI 1.09-12.16, p=0.035) were independent poor prognostic factor for RFS. Conclusion: LR with HAR and OLT had a similar rate of major complications and postoperative mortality. OLT reached higher R0 rates and superior RFS; superior OS could not be demonstrated. [Formula presented]
