N2  - Background: Perihilar cholangiocarcinoma (pCC) is a rare malignancy with poor prognosis. Resectable disease is treated via complex liver resection, whilst in select cases transplantation following neoadjuvant chemoradiation (Mayo protocol) can be offered. The aim of this study was to compare survival outcomes after either liver resection (LR) or transplantation (LT). Methods: Patients undergoing either LT or LR for pCC with curative intent at our center were identified from our prospectively collected liver cancer database (2004 - 2021). Inclusion criteria were 1) a cytology proven diagnosis of pCC, 2) age ≤ 70 3) pathological stage IIIB or less; 4) R0 resection. Disease free and overall survivals were calculated with Kaplan-Meyer methods. Results: 35 patients were included: 18 LT and 17 LR (median age 54 vs 59 years, p=0.38; female sex 22% vs 41%, p=0.27). Pathological stage was significantly lower in the LT group, with 8 (44%) patients having no residual tumor at final pathology (p=0.002). Tumor differentiation and mean number of harvested lymph nodes were similar between the two cohorts (p>0.05). A higher number of patients in the LR group underwent adjuvant treatment (8, 47% vs 2, 11%, p<0.01). After a median follow up of 40 (range 13 - 81) months for LT and 114 (7 - 225) for LR (p=0.001), deaths were 2 (11%) in LT and 11 (65%) in LR, while recurrences were 3 (17%) and 8 (47%) in LR. Five-year actuarial survival rate was 71% in the LT group vs 41% in the LR group (p=0.025). The LT group exhibited a trend toward longer 5-year DFS (80% for LT vs 34% for LR, p = 0.08). Conclusions: Liver transplantation within Mayo protocol is associated with increased overall survival compared to R0 resection in patients with node-negative pCC. These results require further validation through larger prospective studies.
