N2  - Introduction: The World Health Organization (WHO) 2010 classification for combined hepatocellular-cholangiocarcinoma (CHC) describes two main types of CHC: classical subtype (CS) and stem cell subtypes (SC). Although the CS of CHC exhibits earlier recurrence than the stem cell subtype after hepatectomy, there are no data concerning the prognosis after liver transplantation (LT). The aim of our study is to evaluate the long-term prognosis of LT for CHC by WHO 2010 classification-based analysis. Materials and Methods: Between January 2010 and 2019, we identified 30 case of pathologically confirmed CHC who underwent adult LDLT at two tertiary centers. For the analysis, patients with CHC were divided into the CS and SC group. The overall survival (OS) rates were plotted as Kaplan-Meier curves. Univariate and multivariate analyses were performed for risk factors related to OS and recurrence. Results: Among 30 eligible patients, there were 18 (60%) patients with the CS and 12 (40%) patients with SC. There were no significant differences in the demographics, clinical characteristics and preoperative liver function between patients with CS and SC. The OS rate at 1 year (78.5% vs 81.7%), 3 years (62.4% vs 61.3%) and 5 years (53.4% vs 61.3%) was comparable between CS and SC groups (P=0.946). The disease-free survival rates at 1 year (73.1% vs 72.8%), 3 years (54.9% vs 57.9%) and 5 years (47.1% vs 57.9%) was comparable between CS and SC groups (P=0.954). Multivariate analyses revealed that the Child-Pugh score (hazard ratio (HR), 6.95; 95% confidence interval (CI) 1.37-35.2, P=0.019), recurrence (HR 8.11, 95% CI 1.70-38.6, P=0.009), tumor number (HR 2.51, 95% CI 1.99-6.35, P=0.004) and tumor size (HR 1.11, 95% CI 1.03-1.19, P=0.005) were independent risk factor for OS. The tumor number (HR 1.23, 95% CI 1.01-1.52, P=0.05) and tumor size (HR 1.08, 95% CI 1.01-1.16, P=0.024) were identified as a risk factor for recurrence after LT. Conclusion: The OS and recurrence of CHC after LT is comparable between CS and SC groups. The prognosis of patients with CHC undergoing LT seems to be affected not only by the biology of the tumor itself but also by the preoperative recipient state.
