N2  - Background: While “early” recurrence has been used as a surrogate for poor long-term outcomes after resection with curative intent, the definition for “early recurrence” remains controversial for perihilar cholangiocarcinoma (PHCC). The aims of this study were to determine the optimal cut-off for early recurrence and to identify its predictive factors. Methods: Medical records for consecutive patients who underwent radical resection for PHCC from 2004 through 2023 were reviewed. Those who underwent R2 resection, non-radical resection without major liver resection and resection for pM1 disease, and those who received neoadjuvant therapy were excluded. Optimal cut-off for early and late recurrences was determined based on post-recurrence survival (PRS) and disease-specific survival (DSS) using maximal chi-square method. The predictive factors for early recurrence was evaluated using Fisher’s exact test and logistic regression. Results: Two hundred and thirty-three patients were identified and included for the analysis. Recurrence occurred in 132 patients (55%) during the follow-up with median 38 months (IQR 18-65), and median time to recurrence was 15 months. The difference in RFS and DSS (chi-square in Log-rank test) between the patients with “early” and “late” recurrence by the cut-off were shown in Figure 1, and the optimal cut-off was determined as 12 months. Median PRS and DSS were 7 and 15 months for the patients with early recurrence 65 years and nodal involvement were identified (HR 3.8 with p=0.009, HR 3.2 with p=0.001, respectively), while either of operative complexity (right vs left, trisectionectomy vs bisectionectomy, vascular resection, or additional pancreaticoduodenectomy), or the use of adjuvant therapy were not associated with early recurrence. Conclusion: RFS < 12 months is the optimal cut-off to separate the high-risk patients based on subsequent survival. [Formula presented]
