N2  - Purpose: Pre-operative Fluorine-18 fluorodeoxyglucose positron-emission tomography computed tomography (18F-FDG PET-CT) is used for staging in perihilar cholangiocarcinoma (pCCA). We aimed to investigate if pre-operative 18F-FDG PET-CT is a useful predictor of survival in patients undergoing curative-intent resection for pCCA. Methods: All patients who underwent attempted curative resection for pCCA at a single centre over a 13-year period from January 2010-December 2022 were identified from a prospective database. Results of 18F-FDG PET-CT including maximum standardized uptake value (SUVmax) of primary tumour were collected retrospectively. Median SUVmax was used to stratify primary tumours into three groups - high-avidity, low-avidity and no measurable FDG uptake. Disease-free survival (DFS) and overall survival (OS) were assessed using the Kaplan-Meier method. The four variables that had significant survival differences in the univariate analysis were compared using the multivariable Cox proportional hazard regression analysis. All survival analyses were performed using SPSS version 29 (IBM SPSS, IBM Corp.). Results: Ninety patients had attempted surgical resection for pCCA during the study period, of which 71 proceeded to curative-intent resection. 85.9% (61/71) of these patients were investigated with pre-operative 18F-FDG PET-CT. Of these, 34.4% (21/61) did not have measurable FDG-avid primary tumours. Of the FDG-avid primary tumours, median SUVmax was 5.5 (IQR 3). The primary tumour SUVmax ≤5.5 and >5.5 groups included 20 patients each. Five-year disease-free (DFS) and overall survival (OS) in the SUVmax >5.5 group (DFS 8.8%; OS 11.5%) were significantly worse compared to the SUVmax ≤5.5 group (DFS 42.1%; OS 40%)) and the no measurable uptake cohorts (DFS 36.4%; OS 33%). In the multivariate analysis, SUVmax >5.5 was an independent predictor of DFS (p=0.017; HR 3.4 [95% CI 1.2-9.4]) and OS (p=0.016; HR 2.8 [95% CI 1.2-6.4]) in pCCA. Nodal stage (p<0.001; HR 3.6 [95% CI 1.7-7.8])) and margin status (p=0.006; HR 3.2 [95% CI 1.4-7.2]) were also independent predictors of DFS. Conclusions: Over one-third of patients did not have measurable FDG-avid primary tumours. In patients with measurable FDG uptake, SUVmax >5.5 is predictive of poor DFS and OS following curative-intent surgical resection of pCCA. To our knowledge, this is the largest study in a Western cohort of pCCA patients to report this association with survival outcomes. This prognostic information provided by the pre-operative 18F-FDG PET-CT would be useful for counselling patients regarding predicted outcome following major resection for pCCA and could be useful in the future for patient selection for liver transplantation.
